190
00:12:49,858 --> 00:12:59,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, discerning the danger, discerning the extent of sensuality, the extent of its flood, so to speak, as a single phenomenon, not like reminding yourself of all the aspects that you've been through.

191
00:13:00,644 --> 00:13:01,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that would equal safety.

192
00:13:02,305 --> 00:13:06,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, full understanding of gratification, full understanding of danger, means full understanding of safety.

193
00:13:21,403 --> 00:13:25,325
[SPEAKER_03]: Restraint is more primordial than sensuality.

194
00:13:25,745 --> 00:13:27,166
[SPEAKER_03]: That's basically what... Yeah.

195
00:13:27,446 --> 00:13:28,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you think it ties in?

196
00:13:30,008 --> 00:13:37,472
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that's it, because you have to... you do surmount perceptions, sensuality as a whole.

197
00:13:37,512 --> 00:13:40,573
[SPEAKER_03]: You surmount it by discerning it, that whole domain.

198
00:13:42,934 --> 00:13:46,836
[SPEAKER_03]: But you only surmount it through stepping into a bigger phenomenon.

199
00:13:48,257 --> 00:13:49,618
[SPEAKER_01]: But why would it be more primordial?

200
00:13:59,417 --> 00:13:59,677
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry?

201
00:13:59,977 --> 00:14:03,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, more or less.

202
00:14:04,699 --> 00:14:09,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because if it weren't, you wouldn't be able to surmount sensuality.

203
00:14:10,001 --> 00:14:11,322
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to free yourself from it.

204
00:14:13,163 --> 00:14:25,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, what we spoke before, because things are, in the nature, first, asubha, because of the impermanence, because of the non-satisfaction and so on.

205
00:14:26,015 --> 00:14:31,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any notion of sensuality is literally plastered over it, while it's still that the subha is there.

206
00:14:32,717 --> 00:14:42,781
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same way, restraint reveals it, reveals the actual repulsive aspect, even of attractive things.

207
00:14:43,981 --> 00:14:46,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, restraint reveals what's more primordial.

208
00:14:46,622 --> 00:14:53,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, restraint is more primordial, or puts you in touch with what's first, not what's second, which is sensuality.

209
00:14:54,527 --> 00:14:58,429
[SPEAKER_01]: But, of course, you wouldn't arrive there without the restraint just being done first.

210
00:14:58,509 --> 00:14:59,610
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to say no to it.

211
00:14:59,690 --> 00:15:00,311
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know why.

212
00:15:00,351 --> 00:15:01,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know how.

213
00:15:01,091 --> 00:15:01,491
[SPEAKER_01]: It hurts.

214
00:15:02,052 --> 00:15:03,973
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha said there is no dhamma without sīla.

215
00:15:04,353 --> 00:15:05,474
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just start doing it.

216
00:15:06,434 --> 00:15:07,855
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not doing it for its own sake.

217
00:15:07,875 --> 00:15:10,057
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it so to see, so that you can stop doing it.

218
00:15:10,257 --> 00:15:11,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, of course, you will be it.

219
00:15:11,618 --> 00:15:14,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't have to... You won't be stopping anything, basically.

220
00:15:14,599 --> 00:15:16,160
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be your natural mode of behavior.

221
00:15:17,161 --> 00:15:18,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-sensuality and restraint.

222
00:15:23,515 --> 00:15:32,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the repulsive aspect of sensual things is not actually more fundamental than sensuality, freedom from sensuality will not be possible.

223
00:15:37,127 --> 00:15:38,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't fabricate.

224
00:15:39,209 --> 00:15:41,711
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't create the dhamma on top of things like there was nothing.

225
00:15:42,171 --> 00:15:42,312
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

226
00:15:42,352 --> 00:15:43,192
[SPEAKER_01]: The way is out.

227
00:15:43,913 --> 00:15:44,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to discern it.

228
00:15:45,858 --> 00:15:47,960
[SPEAKER_01]: As we said, the path is there, you need to find it.

229
00:15:48,641 --> 00:15:53,765
[SPEAKER_01]: And that path wouldn't be there, if repulsive is not more fundamental than attractive.

230
00:15:54,466 --> 00:15:55,767
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how things are ordered.

231
00:15:56,928 --> 00:15:58,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what you're supposed to discern.

232
00:15:58,269 --> 00:15:59,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you get free from sensuality.

233
00:15:59,911 --> 00:16:02,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by deciding and saying no to it.

234
00:16:04,655 --> 00:16:07,918
[SPEAKER_04]: If anything, it's... is it right to say that sensuality is fabricated?

235
00:16:08,882 --> 00:16:15,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it is, but I don't want to say that, because then people think, okay, all I have to do is stop thinking about my sensuality, and that's how I am fabricated.

236
00:16:15,548 --> 00:16:17,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality is a view.

237
00:16:17,249 --> 00:16:22,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's upādāna, which means the distortion is there, and it's real as such for you.

238
00:16:23,274 --> 00:16:25,055
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, the inversion of perception.

239
00:16:25,696 --> 00:16:28,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, from Arahant's point of view, it's an inversion.

240
00:16:28,698 --> 00:16:30,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana state is an inversion.

241
00:16:30,540 --> 00:16:32,902
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like second putting first, first putting second.

242
00:16:34,005 --> 00:16:36,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But Putujana, that's the only view he has.

243
00:16:36,268 --> 00:16:38,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he has no reference point about first or second.

244
00:16:38,933 --> 00:16:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unless he sees that rightly, in other words, unless he abandons it, he won't see it rightly.

245
00:16:45,362 --> 00:16:49,104
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to... he has to start with that as a starting point.

246
00:16:49,244 --> 00:16:55,548
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't say, oh, well, this sensuality is secondary and the non-sensuality is first, because he doesn't see either.

247
00:16:56,288 --> 00:16:58,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he needs to start... sensuality is first for me.

248
00:16:59,250 --> 00:17:06,574
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mistake, but until I undo that mistake, that is my form of existence, that is my bhāva, that is the extent of my bhāva, the sensual being.

249
00:17:12,137 --> 00:17:12,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see what I mean?

250
00:17:13,077 --> 00:17:14,358
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, it's more primordial.

251
00:17:16,081 --> 00:17:17,202
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, sensual pain, yeah.

252
00:17:17,342 --> 00:17:20,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it reveals the more primordial order, basically.

253
00:17:21,944 --> 00:17:26,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, bhāva means experience, isn't it?

254
00:17:26,626 --> 00:17:26,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhāva?

255
00:17:26,987 --> 00:17:27,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I suppose.

256
00:17:27,747 --> 00:17:30,949
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, being, literally, means the extent of your being.

257
00:17:56,493 --> 00:18:02,544
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you can see it is more non-attraction, generally.

258
00:18:02,985 --> 00:18:03,846
[SPEAKER_03]: But you don't notice that.

259
00:18:04,708 --> 00:18:07,874
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, nice bowl, nice cup or whatever.

260
00:18:08,981 --> 00:18:22,693
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you might notice that, but in order for it to play part in reverse... reverting back the inversion of sensuality, you need to see it... in the same sense, when we started speaking about vaticanupāda, it's the same principle.

261
00:18:23,414 --> 00:18:27,737
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see it as simultaneously present and determining your sensuality.

262
00:18:28,538 --> 00:18:31,060
[SPEAKER_01]: If you see left or right from it, it's a second thing.

263
00:18:32,001 --> 00:18:32,862
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't compare it.

264
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:41,143
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your whole life, your speaking, talking, walking, eating, depends on your beating heart, that you have no say in.

265
00:18:42,304 --> 00:18:50,549
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your central projects, central attractions, central views, central cravings, central pleasures, depend upon presence of things that are actually repulsive.

266
00:18:51,189 --> 00:18:52,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneous presence, not after.

267
00:18:53,631 --> 00:18:55,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that ugly becomes really beautiful.

268
00:18:55,772 --> 00:19:02,015
[SPEAKER_01]: While you're with the beautiful, in its heart, it's ugly, but you're not seeing that relationship between that heart and what you're looking at.

269
00:19:03,656 --> 00:19:04,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're not free from it.

270
00:19:05,610 --> 00:19:08,551
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where you can look at all the asubha pictures you want.

271
00:19:10,692 --> 00:19:13,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can look at one, but if you see the right principle, then you'll know.

272
00:19:13,574 --> 00:19:15,034
[SPEAKER_01]: Then it's like asubha nimitta.

273
00:19:16,615 --> 00:19:22,738
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you recognize, peripherally discern, that signs of, ah, that's where asubha is.

274
00:19:22,838 --> 00:19:23,898
[SPEAKER_01]: You found a place for it.

275
00:19:24,839 --> 00:19:29,201
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not just another object of your eyes and smell and so on.

276
00:19:30,130 --> 00:19:34,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby I will not look at the ugly, I will not look at the beautiful, I will look at the ugly.

277
00:19:34,451 --> 00:19:42,094
[SPEAKER_01]: But I'm still looking and I'm still not understanding the nature of sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, the whole sensual domain you're not understanding.

278
00:19:42,114 --> 00:19:48,476
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just replacing the objects of your eyes, basically, failing to understand the nature of it.

279
00:19:49,416 --> 00:19:51,797
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, yeah, you can, like, which is more?

280
00:19:52,858 --> 00:19:54,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Ugly or beautiful in the world?

281
00:19:54,658 --> 00:19:56,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Just go in the town, which is more?

282
00:19:56,379 --> 00:19:57,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Ugly people or beautiful people?

283
00:19:59,660 --> 00:19:59,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Ugly.

284
00:20:00,899 --> 00:20:01,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Non-attractive.

285
00:20:01,679 --> 00:20:02,920
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the basis for everything.

286
00:20:03,340 --> 00:20:07,821
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you see it in a competitive manner, it's almost like an information.

287
00:20:08,041 --> 00:20:08,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Makes no difference.

288
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:16,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you see it as a basis upon which beautiful exists, to the extent that the basis determines it, then okay.

289
00:20:17,063 --> 00:20:22,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't have the principle of inversion, unless the two things that are inverted simultaneously.

290
00:20:24,423 --> 00:20:26,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Inversion is not one, then the other.

291
00:20:26,045 --> 00:20:26,705
[SPEAKER_01]: It's one and two.

292
00:20:26,885 --> 00:20:31,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Inversion is what's first, it's second, what's second, it's first, while both are still there.

293
00:20:32,090 --> 00:20:33,551
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a definition of inversion.

294
00:20:38,396 --> 00:20:45,062
[SPEAKER_01]: My solar inverter, it inverts the electricity that comes to another electricity that you can use, simultaneously.

295
00:20:45,422 --> 00:20:48,745
[SPEAKER_01]: If the electricity that comes disappears, there is no inversion anymore.

296
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:52,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to have two things for that.

297
00:20:53,122 --> 00:20:54,903
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to stop mixing the order up.

298
00:20:55,384 --> 00:20:59,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for putrujana, or for someone who's not free from sensual being, first is beautiful.

299
00:21:00,448 --> 00:21:01,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful is first.

300
00:21:02,990 --> 00:21:05,131
[SPEAKER_01]: But you won't find ugly as second.

301
00:21:05,191 --> 00:21:08,174
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to find it within the heart of that first is beautiful.

302
00:21:09,712 --> 00:21:17,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Without abandoning first the beautiful, you need to see its foundations are actually fully dependent, determined, rooted in the ugly, the repulsive.

303
00:21:18,314 --> 00:21:22,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you... that's how it stops being ugly and beautiful.

304
00:21:22,895 --> 00:21:25,295
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes really ugly and slightly less ugly.

305
00:21:26,155 --> 00:21:28,036
[SPEAKER_01]: But all it's repulsive, all of it.

306
00:21:30,156 --> 00:21:35,977
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the... similar principle, but that's what I think maybe Sariputta, the Buddha said, um...

307
00:21:37,538 --> 00:21:39,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure counts as pain,

308
00:21:40,003 --> 00:21:42,025
[SPEAKER_01]: In the discipline of the Noble One... Sariputta.

309
00:21:42,165 --> 00:21:42,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Sariputta.

310
00:21:42,865 --> 00:21:46,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure counts as pain, and pain counts like a deadly arrow.

311
00:21:46,789 --> 00:21:52,173
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, it's all bad.

312
00:21:53,214 --> 00:21:56,677
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all bad, but doesn't pertain to you anymore, because you abandoned it.

313
00:21:59,899 --> 00:22:02,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like that you need to be like, oh, it's all bad.

314
00:22:06,284 --> 00:22:10,366
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it is.

315
00:22:10,386 --> 00:22:13,248
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, sure.

316
00:22:13,488 --> 00:22:13,808
[SPEAKER_01]: I agree.

317
00:22:13,828 --> 00:22:18,291
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I qualified it, you don't want to say that, because usually implies, ah, fabrication.

318
00:22:18,331 --> 00:22:19,812
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, I'm fabricating it now.

319
00:22:20,252 --> 00:22:21,893
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is stop fabricating it.

320
00:22:22,493 --> 00:22:22,613
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

321
00:22:22,754 --> 00:22:24,454
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is uproot that thing.

322
00:22:25,275 --> 00:22:30,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you won't be able to fabricate sensuality, even if you want to, because you uprooted the inversion.

323
00:22:31,758 --> 00:22:36,839
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, for anāgāmī and arahant, it's not a matter of choice that they can't engage in sensual behavior.

324
00:22:37,119 --> 00:22:38,320
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the state of their being.

325
00:22:40,100 --> 00:22:46,062
[SPEAKER_02]: You get a lot of these weird translations of, like, saṅkhara, like, fabricating the conditioned reality.

326
00:22:46,322 --> 00:22:47,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I mean.

327
00:22:47,962 --> 00:22:50,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I heard even more flamboyant translations than that.

328
00:22:52,582 --> 00:22:58,347
[SPEAKER_04]: No, if anything, because of ignorance, fabrication is like... it's already there.

329
00:22:59,688 --> 00:23:00,389
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole thing.

330
00:23:00,769 --> 00:23:02,611
[SPEAKER_01]: All of it is already there for Putujana.

331
00:23:02,651 --> 00:23:03,952
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to reverse the order.

332
00:23:04,573 --> 00:23:05,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Anuloma, Patiloma.

333
00:23:06,395 --> 00:23:07,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Part of the same things.

334
00:23:11,599 --> 00:23:17,525
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but what I was trying to mean earlier was that I've noticed that when...

335
00:23:18,620 --> 00:23:24,101
[SPEAKER_04]: Beautiful, what I consider to be beautiful, start to become ugly.

336
00:23:24,121 --> 00:23:25,661
[SPEAKER_04]: I would try to look for beautiful in that.

337
00:23:26,201 --> 00:23:26,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

338
00:23:27,522 --> 00:23:27,622
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure.

339
00:23:27,662 --> 00:23:31,923
[SPEAKER_01]: I would try to build up... Because the inversion principle is where you act from.

340
00:23:32,503 --> 00:23:33,143
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the assumption.

341
00:23:33,183 --> 00:23:35,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's basically the central upādāna.

342
00:23:36,163 --> 00:23:36,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly that.

343
00:23:37,044 --> 00:23:40,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Where your experience is actually showing you ugly, left and right.

344
00:23:40,684 --> 00:23:43,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you keep discerning only beautiful within it, because that's all you see.

345
00:23:44,706 --> 00:23:45,547
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's your doing.

346
00:23:45,647 --> 00:23:48,949
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, the beautiful things in the world are not sensuality.

347
00:23:49,389 --> 00:23:53,512
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your thought and lust in regard to it, that man's sensuality is there.

348
00:23:56,333 --> 00:23:57,034
[SPEAKER_03]: That's your doing.

349
00:23:57,634 --> 00:23:58,335
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, exactly.

350
00:23:58,355 --> 00:24:01,117
[SPEAKER_03]: But the asubha is not your doing.

351
00:24:01,237 --> 00:24:02,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Asubha is not your doing.

352
00:24:02,237 --> 00:24:06,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Asubha is recognition that undermines your own doing of sensuality.

353
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:07,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can't do it.

354
00:24:10,407 --> 00:24:14,708
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in order to fully undermine, you need to start seeing the danger in what you were doing or what you want to do.

355
00:24:15,289 --> 00:24:24,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't, you're still going to be attracted to it.

356
00:24:24,711 --> 00:24:28,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's a form of doing in the level we spoke about.

357
00:24:28,192 --> 00:24:30,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, you know, doing, sweeping the leaves or something.

358
00:24:31,734 --> 00:24:32,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Just even on a mental level.

359
00:25:22,342 --> 00:25:34,969
[SPEAKER_02]: It reminds me of the idea that when I attend something, I think Jnanavīr mentioned, like, the mind as its object can attend, he called them, like, universals, or natures of things, so to speak.

360
00:25:35,449 --> 00:25:40,351
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I was confused by my definition, because, like, let's say, being.

361
00:25:40,952 --> 00:25:45,634
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't, I can't obviously attend being, like, I attend stuff, room.

362
00:25:46,254 --> 00:25:47,295
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how I thought in

363
00:25:55,747 --> 00:26:05,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first, just, when you say when you attend something, then that paticampara would be that thing you're attending is determined by something else.

364
00:26:05,752 --> 00:26:14,075
[SPEAKER_01]: That's true as a principle, but in practice, that thing that determines what you're attending is behind your attention, so to speak.

365
00:26:14,396 --> 00:26:18,758
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, it's not something you can also attend, because if you do, you make it into another thing.

366
00:26:19,858 --> 00:26:21,659
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm insisting on this, because if you start

367
00:26:22,993 --> 00:26:28,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Tempting, at least, to see that is, you cannot not include yourself in that experience.

368
00:26:28,276 --> 00:26:31,298
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it ceases to be abstract, pondering with this, this.

369
00:26:31,838 --> 00:26:33,279
[SPEAKER_01]: You are part of that picture.

370
00:26:33,339 --> 00:26:37,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Your experience, right here, right now, it's part of what you're trying to understand.

371
00:26:38,041 --> 00:26:44,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I'm attending at this, and it's determined by something else that I cannot attend, it's simultaneously there, which is behind my attention.

372
00:26:45,606 --> 00:26:47,507
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I can't just, okay, so what is that thing?

373
00:26:48,272 --> 00:26:50,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if I attend to it, it's behind.

374
00:26:50,593 --> 00:26:52,735
[SPEAKER_01]: It's behind from the direction of your attention.

375
00:26:54,235 --> 00:26:55,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's behind?

376
00:26:55,176 --> 00:26:57,758
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that peripheral that I talk about, that you can discern.

377
00:26:58,178 --> 00:27:04,141
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you attend the cup, you can see there is feeling present, there is intention present, without, you know, stopping your attention to the cup.

378
00:27:05,502 --> 00:27:08,764
[SPEAKER_01]: You see there's a decision present, there are views present, basically.

379
00:27:08,884 --> 00:27:11,145
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at them directly, they become a new thing.

380
00:27:11,926 --> 00:27:13,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means something else is then peripheral to it.

381
00:27:15,087 --> 00:27:16,767
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't have it both to the same extent.

382
00:27:17,147 --> 00:27:22,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's perfectly fine to contemplate that principle on the level of senses.

383
00:27:22,769 --> 00:27:38,492
[SPEAKER_01]: What you refer to in terms of the being and the more refined ways of the same principle, would be once you are just fully discerning the peripheral in regard to your attending, then you can attend to that peripheral in that non-attentive manner, so to speak, indirectly.

384
00:27:39,852 --> 00:27:42,593
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what it means like obtaining jhānas at will.

385
00:27:43,453 --> 00:27:47,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know exactly where they are in relation to establishing your mind.

386
00:27:48,156 --> 00:27:51,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't establish them just through perception, or through looking, or through doing something.

387
00:27:51,979 --> 00:27:59,985
[SPEAKER_01]: But because you became so skilled in discerning the peripheral domain of mindfulness, you could just literally attend it in that indirect manner, and that's it.

388
00:28:01,691 --> 00:28:07,396
[SPEAKER_02]: You can see it without having to look at it.

389
00:28:08,396 --> 00:28:09,377
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm talking about.

390
00:28:09,678 --> 00:28:14,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you practice that, the more you get to see without having to attend it and look at it directly.

391
00:28:15,423 --> 00:28:23,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's what I mean the problem of Puthujjana and the notion of his being is it's real what I can look, what I can perceive, the problem of method, what I can do.

392
00:28:27,065 --> 00:28:32,187
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, for as long as he's focused on the content of what he's perceiving, he's not going to see the nature of it.

393
00:28:32,247 --> 00:28:34,008
[SPEAKER_01]: And nature is that peripheral I referred to.

394
00:28:37,289 --> 00:28:40,190
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said knowledge comes after perception.

395
00:28:40,530 --> 00:28:41,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, you can understand it.

396
00:28:42,111 --> 00:28:44,832
[SPEAKER_01]: You collect the information of perception, then you figure out the knowledge.

397
00:28:44,992 --> 00:28:47,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can also understand it in a simultaneous manner.

398
00:28:47,213 --> 00:28:47,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It's after.

399
00:28:48,513 --> 00:28:52,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Like this is perceived, and it's all over based on that, but it's not it.

400
00:28:53,555 --> 00:28:54,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowledge is not the perception.

401
00:28:55,676 --> 00:28:56,416
[SPEAKER_01]: It comes after it.

402
00:28:58,156 --> 00:29:18,125
[SPEAKER_02]: So, can we say then, with the example I gave, that I would, eh... recognize, let's use that word instead of attend, a phenomena of being, and see implicit in it, so to say, the assumptions of... are simultaneously present to you?

403
00:29:18,286 --> 00:29:18,566
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

404
00:29:19,206 --> 00:29:19,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

405
00:29:19,906 --> 00:29:25,149
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you won't recognize it if you start thinking, oh, I should be recognizing the phenomenon of being.

406
00:29:26,109 --> 00:29:26,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's discerned.

407
00:29:27,430 --> 00:29:27,630
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

408
00:29:28,105 --> 00:29:32,708
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the information you have about what you're supposed to discern, it's going to be recognized then.

409
00:29:34,229 --> 00:29:41,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you dwell on the principle, sufficiently enough, you start discerning, okay, that's what it means.

410
00:29:41,693 --> 00:29:47,116
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you take it as an object of, okay, the being must recognize, but it won't work like that.

411
00:29:47,196 --> 00:29:48,397
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the level of abstract.

412
00:29:48,477 --> 00:29:49,458
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference.

413
00:29:50,378 --> 00:29:53,900
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you can't think about your being, because you can only be your being.

414
00:29:54,121 --> 00:29:54,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

415
00:29:55,221 --> 00:29:55,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

416
00:29:56,718 --> 00:30:00,621
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all the problems of the Western classical and existential philosophy.

417
00:30:00,661 --> 00:30:01,001
[SPEAKER_01]: They just...

418
00:30:02,442 --> 00:30:04,723
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no being without thinking, but you can't think you're being.

419
00:30:06,505 --> 00:30:15,271
[SPEAKER_01]: From... from Decartes to Sartre, they couldn't figure it out, because they took this sense of self for granted.

420
00:30:15,351 --> 00:30:21,135
[SPEAKER_02]: It's almost like you said, the order, like you see your thinking as secondary to the being, rightly speaking.

421
00:30:21,335 --> 00:30:23,736
[SPEAKER_02]: You can only think about your being, because you're being.

422
00:30:24,517 --> 00:30:25,858
[SPEAKER_01]: But without thinking, you wouldn't be.

423
00:30:31,494 --> 00:30:33,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it determines each other, basically.

424
00:30:34,555 --> 00:30:35,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking means you are.

425
00:30:36,155 --> 00:30:37,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You are because you think.

426
00:30:38,376 --> 00:30:43,737
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a valid recognition of Decartes and others, but now you need to.

427
00:30:43,897 --> 00:30:46,138
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the dhamma starts.

428
00:30:46,198 --> 00:30:47,338
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the philosophy ended.

429
00:30:48,638 --> 00:30:49,939
[SPEAKER_01]: They couldn't go further than that.

430
00:30:50,739 --> 00:30:51,699
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's where dhamma begins.

431
00:30:51,739 --> 00:30:53,019
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where dhamma goes beyond.

432
00:30:53,059 --> 00:30:54,280
[SPEAKER_01]: It's ātaka vācāra.

433
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:55,260
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't think it.

434
00:30:56,378 --> 00:30:58,219
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, it doesn't mean it's mystical.

435
00:30:58,719 --> 00:31:00,820
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's not on the level of reasoning and logic.

436
00:31:01,261 --> 00:31:03,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that already is secondary to the being.

437
00:31:11,606 --> 00:31:13,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's get back to practical manners... matters.

438
00:31:15,928 --> 00:31:18,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all this would be extension of the same thing I'm talking about.

439
00:31:20,611 --> 00:31:24,513
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't need to... you don't need to go to it through... through philosophical ponderings.

440
00:31:31,716 --> 00:31:33,297
[SPEAKER_02]: The notion that for me...

441
00:31:33,357 --> 00:31:35,579
[SPEAKER_01]: I just... as I did when you asked about paticampada.

442
00:31:35,959 --> 00:31:39,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can think in that manner and you could say, yeah, it's correct according to principle.

443
00:31:40,382 --> 00:31:42,823
[SPEAKER_01]: But, to some extent, you're leaving yourself out.

444
00:31:43,423 --> 00:31:47,586
[SPEAKER_01]: As in your sense of self, right here, right now, in this experience as a whole, it's not included.

445
00:31:48,347 --> 00:31:55,131
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way to include it is to start discerning the peripheral that's peripheral to your sense of self as well, not just to the object of your attention.

446
00:31:56,210 --> 00:31:59,093
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you will... you will force yourself to be included.

447
00:31:59,613 --> 00:32:02,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means that any discernment, any understanding that happens, will happen on that level.

448
00:32:03,717 --> 00:32:08,802
[SPEAKER_01]: And it will be measured by the feeling and absence of... of... of dukkha, if done rightly.

449
00:32:09,602 --> 00:32:18,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, it's not that you make understanding of impermanence and non-control pleasurable or... or... or resulting in freedom.

450
00:32:18,931 --> 00:32:20,733
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just how things... things are.

451
00:32:21,597 --> 00:32:25,740
[SPEAKER_01]: If things were not truly impermanent, again, freedom from suffering would not be possible.

452
00:32:26,040 --> 00:32:35,345
[SPEAKER_01]: But they are truly impermanent, so freedom from suffering is possible, but only for those who discern it.

453
00:32:39,588 --> 00:32:51,075
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean, like, the measure of your understanding is inability to suffer until you become completely an arahant, of course, by which time you will be quite

454
00:32:53,262 --> 00:32:54,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite certain and quite confident in it.

455
00:32:55,624 --> 00:33:07,974
[SPEAKER_01]: But even before arhantship, the absence of suffering, not because you're avoiding it, not because you're managing it, not because you're very skilled at managing, not because nothing challenges you, but because you're unable to suffer.

456
00:33:08,395 --> 00:33:15,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Amid suffering, suffering becomes inconceivable.

457
00:33:20,818 --> 00:33:23,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to do anything on account of it, because it didn't touch you in the first place.

458
00:33:26,102 --> 00:33:28,324
[SPEAKER_01]: What we spoke a couple of weeks ago, the arrow.

459
00:33:29,905 --> 00:33:32,688
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't grieve, you don't lament, because it didn't touch you in the first place.

460
00:33:40,194 --> 00:33:42,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But that didn't touch you in the first place, it's not an accident.

461
00:33:43,985 --> 00:33:46,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you stumble upon or something that happened to you.

462
00:33:46,586 --> 00:33:47,727
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something you discerned.

463
00:33:48,087 --> 00:33:50,608
[SPEAKER_01]: You discerned the place where it won't touch you in the first place.

464
00:33:51,429 --> 00:33:51,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering.

465
00:33:52,930 --> 00:33:57,172
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, whatever is to be obtained by manly effort, manly reflection, manly discipline, is our thing.

466
00:33:57,212 --> 00:33:58,072
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha would say.

467
00:33:59,173 --> 00:33:59,773
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not given.

468
00:33:59,833 --> 00:34:00,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not...

469
00:34:00,514 --> 00:34:01,894
[SPEAKER_01]: It hasn't occurred.

470
00:34:02,215 --> 00:34:03,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I had this experience.

471
00:34:03,335 --> 00:34:04,616
[SPEAKER_01]: It must mean I'm free from suffering.

472
00:34:18,583 --> 00:34:32,235
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I don't know how people individually think, but based on what I've seen, whenever this passage will be quoted, people would perceive unremitting effort means I determine it.

473
00:34:32,935 --> 00:34:35,678
[SPEAKER_01]: I determine it so hard that I'll never give it up.

474
00:34:36,138 --> 00:34:36,779
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

475
00:34:36,819 --> 00:34:37,099
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not.

476
00:34:37,179 --> 00:34:39,261
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a result of your will and volition.

477
00:34:40,141 --> 00:34:41,663
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a result of your understanding.

478
00:34:42,190 --> 00:34:45,233
[SPEAKER_01]: That establishes your mind into the unwavering effort.

479
00:34:45,553 --> 00:34:49,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you know, yeah, my seniors can go wherever, effort won't cease.

480
00:34:50,198 --> 00:34:54,963
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, a monk would establish his mind upon the perception of the forest, then go to the village.

481
00:34:54,983 --> 00:34:59,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever happens in the village doesn't overwhelm his mind, because his mind is established upon the greater perception.

482
00:35:00,268 --> 00:35:03,451
[SPEAKER_01]: So, establish your mind upon the perception of unwavering effort.

483
00:35:04,130 --> 00:35:07,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Discern it as an independent phenomena, independent of your doing.

484
00:35:08,373 --> 00:35:17,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing, means whatever has the nature of arising, has arisen independently of your sense of self, including your very effort.

485
00:35:18,379 --> 00:35:22,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you need to do it first, in order to discern it as a phenomena.

486
00:35:22,162 --> 00:35:25,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you do, then that unremitting effort, it's effortless.

487
00:35:26,324 --> 00:35:26,985
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not doing it.

488
00:35:28,806 --> 00:35:31,508
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you thought you were doing it, because you thought you could do it.

489
00:35:50,293 --> 00:35:51,679
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the fire-breakage process.

490
00:35:51,920 --> 00:35:52,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty much.

491
00:36:46,269 --> 00:36:46,769
That's it.

492
00:37:19,175 --> 00:37:23,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

493
00:37:23,700 --> 00:37:24,441
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's true.

494
00:37:24,461 --> 00:37:30,168
[SPEAKER_02]: You just kind of, in a way of speaking, find yourself efforting, in a way of speaking.

495
00:37:31,210 --> 00:37:35,074
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess you could say you bring your mind to it, but it endures as long as it does.

496
00:37:35,294 --> 00:37:46,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you start losing the... losing the perception of the phenomenon of effort enduring, that's where you, like, we all, we would say, arise energy, for the unreasonable states.

497
00:37:46,886 --> 00:37:49,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But once they're there, you know, I'm rising up.

498
00:37:49,589 --> 00:37:52,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Now they're there, let them endure, because you established it.

499
00:37:53,012 --> 00:37:54,314
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you establish the mind.

500
00:38:04,696 --> 00:38:06,537
[SPEAKER_01]: And how long it lasts, that's not up to you.

501
00:38:07,117 --> 00:38:13,120
[SPEAKER_01]: That's pretty much predetermined by the clarity of your discernment and and that effort.

502
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:14,900
[SPEAKER_01]: No pun intended.

503
00:38:16,141 --> 00:38:18,602
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's said in that... was it Chula Vedala?

504
00:38:20,204 --> 00:38:25,711
[SPEAKER_01]: About the jhāna, it says, oh, a monk doesn't think, I'm about to attain jhāna, or I'm getting into jhāna.

505
00:38:25,971 --> 00:38:30,437
[SPEAKER_01]: His mind gets set beforehand, established, and then the jhāna is established.

506
00:38:30,877 --> 00:38:37,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And it lasts for as long as that previous sort of setup has determined it to last.

507
00:38:37,866 --> 00:38:38,507
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not up to him.

508
00:38:50,112 --> 00:39:01,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, yes, but you're going to go through some pain of, like, you know, saying no to the unwholesome and so on, like having to endure the discrepancy, say no to the pull.

509
00:39:02,340 --> 00:39:08,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, once you start discerning that as a phenomenon, then it becomes effortless.

510
00:39:09,085 --> 00:39:13,628
[SPEAKER_01]: In the ultimate sense, from Arahant's point of view, it's effortless.

511
00:39:14,623 --> 00:39:17,585
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, Putrajana starts from the other end, and that is his reality.

512
00:39:18,365 --> 00:39:25,269
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's gonna take some, like, actual doing of saying no, withstanding things, saying no to the pull, and so on.

513
00:39:25,729 --> 00:39:29,511
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, doing it for its own sake won't result in any liberation.

514
00:39:30,311 --> 00:39:34,954
[SPEAKER_01]: But doing it and then understanding the nature of that doing, that can result.

515
00:39:35,734 --> 00:39:36,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it becomes effortless.

516
00:39:36,795 --> 00:39:37,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you're not doing it.

517
00:39:38,135 --> 00:39:40,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you're not doing sīla nor your duty of a monk.

518
00:39:41,710 --> 00:39:42,270
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not done.

519
00:39:42,490 --> 00:39:43,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there, but you are not doing it.

520
00:39:44,411 --> 00:39:48,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sīla bhātta paramāsa is gone.

521
00:39:48,533 --> 00:39:49,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla really want to develop that.

522
00:39:51,635 --> 00:39:51,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

523
00:39:52,055 --> 00:39:53,776
[SPEAKER_01]: I think that's what we recorded, pretty much, wasn't it?

524
00:39:55,296 --> 00:39:56,297
[SPEAKER_04]: I was mentioning the same things.

525
00:39:56,597 --> 00:39:57,878
[SPEAKER_01]: In that, like, non-activity.

526
00:39:58,578 --> 00:39:58,798
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

527
00:39:59,158 --> 00:40:00,239
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what I was thinking as well.

528
00:40:00,399 --> 00:40:00,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

529
00:40:00,779 --> 00:40:01,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

530
00:40:01,819 --> 00:40:02,960
[SPEAKER_04]: I thought, actually, like,

531
00:40:04,114 --> 00:40:08,858
[SPEAKER_04]: Unlimiting effort sounds pretty much on the same level as non-activity.

532
00:40:09,239 --> 00:40:09,819
[SPEAKER_01]: It is, yeah.

533
00:40:10,740 --> 00:40:13,503
[SPEAKER_01]: If you discern non-activity correctly, that is effortless.

534
00:40:14,043 --> 00:40:16,325
[SPEAKER_01]: But your state is the state of established effort.

535
00:40:17,466 --> 00:40:21,170
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you need to establish something until it takes root, until it takes place.

536
00:40:21,710 --> 00:40:23,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't have to, because it's there.

537
00:40:45,545 --> 00:40:50,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Any discernment of the phenomena, it's pretty much indirect.

538
00:40:50,986 --> 00:40:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't attend to it.

539
00:40:51,787 --> 00:40:53,707
[SPEAKER_01]: As you said, you can't attend to the being of a thing.

540
00:40:54,427 --> 00:40:55,888
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern it.

541
00:40:56,028 --> 00:41:01,309
[SPEAKER_01]: But, once you start discerning, you realize, well, it's directly indirect, basically.

542
00:41:01,549 --> 00:41:03,150
[SPEAKER_01]: It's direct knowledge of the indirect way.

543
00:41:04,710 --> 00:41:07,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to go through these indirect motions that will result into it.

544
00:41:07,811 --> 00:41:10,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's only the beginning, because you're looking at the wrong place.

545
00:41:15,313 --> 00:41:19,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, against the grain is indirect, from with the grain of puthujjana.

546
00:41:19,623 --> 00:41:23,852
[SPEAKER_01]: But once against the grain becomes your mode of being, that's the direct mode of being.

547
00:41:23,872 --> 00:41:25,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And you abandon the with the grain.

548
00:41:26,129 --> 00:41:30,090
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the result of that discernment is abandonment of these things.

549
00:41:30,190 --> 00:41:31,351
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you can't...

550
00:41:31,371 --> 00:41:35,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, you know, I'm anāgāmī, I had a good time, now I want to go back and do what Bhuddha said.

551
00:41:35,692 --> 00:41:35,832
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

552
00:41:35,872 --> 00:41:37,293
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes... it's abandoned.

553
00:41:37,673 --> 00:41:41,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the other way became your mode of being and it's irreversible.

554
00:41:41,614 --> 00:41:41,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

555
00:41:41,894 --> 00:41:44,375
[SPEAKER_01]: Because ignorance was never supposed to be there.

556
00:41:44,855 --> 00:41:52,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It's beginningless, but there is no first point from which ignorance wasn't and then it became, as the Buddha said himself.

557
00:41:53,318 --> 00:41:55,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you undo it, it cannot take root again.

558
00:42:01,337 --> 00:42:06,540
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there is a slight mimic of abandonment in restraint.

559
00:42:08,121 --> 00:42:15,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you understand the nature of restraint, without understanding, it won't happen by accident.

560
00:42:16,946 --> 00:42:18,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding can't happen by accident.

561
00:42:29,387 --> 00:42:30,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Referring to abandonment.

562
00:42:30,649 --> 00:42:36,020
